art-1quater:
Definition and status of autonomous entities 
Within certain States, autonomous entities are constituted in order to guarantee the cultural, educational and municipal rights of minority communities. 
Autonomous entities hold their own competences in matters of: 
education in the minority language and adapted school curricula; 
cultural institutions, media and community libraries; 
management of ordinary municipal affairs within their territorial perimeter; 
preservation and promotion of the cultural and religious heritage of the community; 
cross-border cooperation with kindred communities in neighbouring States. 
Autonomous entities have no legislative power of their own. They exercise their competences within the framework of the laws of the State to which they are attached and of the Confederal Constitution. 
Autonomous entities have a right to be consulted on any decision of the State that directly affects the interests of their community.

art-38:
Attachment 
Attachment is determined by the municipality of origin of the person, of one of his parents or of one of his grandparents; failing that, by his municipality of residence within the territory of the Confederation. It is not a matter of free choice. 
The municipalities of the occupied territories, claimed by a confederated State and administered by a third country, establish attachment to that State in the same way as other municipalities. 
Confederal law regulates: 
the attachment of minors by descent and by adoption; 
the period of residence required where attachment rests on residence alone; 
change of attachment, which may not result from free choice; 
the keeping of the register of citizens.

art-41:
Social goals 
The Confederation and the States, as a complement to personal responsibility and private initiative, endeavour to ensure that: 
every person has access to social security; 
every person has access to the health care that he requires; 
families are protected and encouraged as communities of adults and children; 
every person capable of working can earn his living by working under fair conditions; 
every person seeking accommodation can find, for himself and his family, appropriate accommodation on reasonable terms; 
children and young people, as well as persons of working age, can obtain an education and undergo basic and advanced training in accordance with their abilities; 
children and young people are encouraged to become independent and socially responsible people and are supported in their social, cultural and political integration. 
The Confederation and the States endeavour to ensure that every person is protected against the economic consequences of old age, invalidity, illness, accident, unemployment, maternity, being orphaned and being widowed. 
They endeavour to achieve these social goals within the scope of their constitutional powers and the resources available to them. 
No direct right to State benefits may be established on the basis of these social goals.

art-49:
Declaration of general application and requirement to participate 
At the request of interested States, the Confederation may declare inter-Balkan agreements to be generally binding or require States to participate in inter-Balkan agreements in the following areas: 
the execution of criminal penalties and measures; 
school education, in the matters specified in Article 64; 
higher education institutions (Article 66); 
cultural institutions of supra-regional importance; 
waste management; 
waste water treatment; 
urban transport; 
advanced medical science and specialist clinics; 
institutions for the rehabilitation and care of persons with disabilities. 
The declaration of general application and the requirement to participate are made by the Kongres in the form of a confederal decree. 
The law specifies the requirements for the declaration of general application and for the requirement to participate, and regulates the procedure.

art-87:
Protection of animals 
The Confederation legislates on the protection of animals. 
It regulates in particular: 
the keeping and care of animals; 
experiments on animals and procedures carried out on living animals; 
the use of animals; 
the import of animals and animal products; 
the trade in animals and the transport of animals; 
the slaughter of animals. 
The confederated States are responsible for the implementation of the relevant confederal regulations, except where the law reserves this duty for the Confederation.

art-94:
Fund for confederal roads and transport infrastructure 
The funding of the confederal roads and of contributions to measures to improve transport infrastructure in cities and conurbations that is related to road traffic is provided by a fund. 
The fund is financed from the following resources: 
the net revenue from the charge for the use of confederal roads under Article 93; 
the net revenue from the special consumption tax on motor fuels; 
the net revenue from the surcharge on motor vehicles; 
the net revenue from charges related to the use of road infrastructure; 
a share of the net revenue from the consumption tax levied on all motor fuels other than aviation fuels; this share corresponds to nine per cent of the resources specified in letter c and to nine per cent of half of the net revenue from the consumption tax levied on all motor fuels other than aviation fuels, but at most sixty billion dinars per year; its indexation is regulated by law; 
as a rule, ten per cent of the net revenue from the consumption tax levied on all motor fuels other than aviation fuels; 
revenue from the special financing and the contributions of the confederated States to compensate for the additional expenditure arising from the inclusion of new sections in the confederal road network; 
other resources allocated by law that are related to road traffic. 
A special financing account is kept for the following tasks and expenditure related to road traffic: 
contributions to measures promoting combined transport and the transport of accompanied motor vehicles; 
contributions to the costs of State roads; 
contributions to protective structures against natural hazards and to measures for environmental and landscape protection made necessary by road traffic; 
general contributions to the costs incurred by the confederated States for roads open to motor vehicles; 
contributions to confederated States without confederal roads; 
research and administration; 
contributions to the fund. 
Half of the net revenue from the consumption tax levied on all motor fuels other than aviation fuels is credited to the special financing, after deduction of the resources specified in the second paragraph, letter e. 
If there is a proven need in the special financing and in order to build up appropriate reserves within it, the revenue from the consumption tax may be credited to the special financing instead of being allocated to the fund.

art-100:
Organs of the Confederation 
The organs of the Confederation are: 
the Kongres (legislative organ); 
the Narodni Sabor (Assembly of Nations); 
the Konfederalno Veće (Confederal Council — executive organ); 
the Ustavni Sud (Constitutional Court — judicial organ).

art-102:
Constituent peoples 
The Confederation recognises seven constituent peoples: 
the Slovenes (Slovenci); 
the Croats (Hrvati); 
the Serbs (Srbi); 
the Bosniaks (Bošñaci); 
the Montenegrins (Crnogorci); 
the Macedonians (Makedonci); 
the Bulgarians (Bãlgari). 
Every citizen of the Confederation who has reached the age of eighteen may register in the national register of the people of his choice. This choice is personal and voluntary. No one may be compelled to declare a national affiliation or suffer prejudice by reason of that choice. 
No one may register in another national register before a period of fifteen years has elapsed since his previous registration. Withdrawal from a register is free; it does not allow registration in another register before that period has elapsed. 
A first registration is probationary for three years: it may be rectified once, in favour of another register. The rectification takes effect under the conditions of the following paragraph; the period of fifteen years runs from the rectification. 
A registration or a change of register takes effect, for the election of the Narodni Sabor and eligibility to it, only from the first election held more than one year after the application. 
Citizens who do not wish to register in any national register retain all of their civic rights. They take part in elections to the Kongres and in referendums but do not vote for the Narodni Sabor. 
Where the Constitution refers to a person's belonging to a constituent people, it means his registration in the national register of that people.

art-105:
Competences of the Kongres 
The Kongres: 
enacts confederal laws; 
adopts the confederal budget; 
ratifies international treaties; 
authorises the deployment of the army; 
elects, jointly with the Narodni Sabor, the members of the Konfederalno Veće; 
elects the judges of the Ustavni Sud on the proposal of the Narodni Sabor; 
exercises general oversight over the Konfederalno Veće and the confederal administration; 
grants pardons; 
grants the guarantee to the constitutions of the States (Article 52); 
holds the other elections assigned to it by the Constitution.

art-106bis:
Initiative and promulgation of laws 
The right of legislative initiative belongs: 
to the Konfederalno Veće; 
to each delegate to the Kongres; 
to each national delegation of the Narodni Sabor; 
to at least eight States, of which no more than half belong to the same constituent people (Article 1 bis). 
Bills are tabled before the Kongres. 
The President of the Confederation promulgates confederal laws and has them published in the Official Journal within thirty days of their final adoption. He may not oppose them.

art-109:
Competences of the Narodni Sabor 
The Narodni Sabor exercises the following competences: 
a. Right of mandatory consent. Confederal laws on the following matters may not enter into force without the approval of the Narodni Sabor: 
languages and the alphabet; 
education and national school curricula; 
culture and the protection of national heritage; 
the symbols of the Confederation and of the States; 
confederal memorial policy, including official commemorations, confederal public holidays, and the naming of institutions, infrastructure and places under the Confederation; 
changes to State borders; 
the status of autonomous entities; 
the rights of the constituent peoples; 
the list of confederal crimes and the competences of the confederal security forces; 
the confederal financial equalisation formula; 
constitutional amendments. 
International treaties concerning these matters are subject to the same right of consent. In the event of refusal, the conciliation procedure of Article 110 ter applies. 
b. Alarm-bell procedure. For any other confederal law, if two thirds of the members of a national delegation vote an alarm motion within thirty days of the adoption of the law by the Kongres, the law is suspended and referred to the conciliation committee (Article 110 ter). If conciliation fails, the law may be adopted only by a two-thirds majority of the Kongres. 
c. Nomination of candidates. Each national delegation proposes one candidate to the Konfederalno Veće, three candidates for each seat of its people to be filled on the Ustavni Sud, and three candidates to the Confederal Court of Audit. 
d. Petitions of autonomous entities. The Narodni Sabor receives and examines the petitions of autonomous entities. 
e. Advisory opinion. The Narodni Sabor may issue an opinion on any matter of confederal interest.

art-114:
Confederal departments 
Each member of the Konfederalno Veće heads a confederal department. The departments are: 
Foreign Affairs (seat: Sarajevo); 
Defence and Security (seat: Sofija); 
Finance and the Treasury (seat: Beograd); 
Justice and Fundamental Rights (seat: Zagreb); 
Education, Culture and Sciences (seat: Sofija); 
Economy and Infrastructure (seat: Beograd); 
Environment, Territory and Resources (seat: Sarajevo). 
The allocation of departments among the councillors is decided collegially within the Konfederalno Veće. No nation may head the same department for two consecutive terms. 
Each councillor heads his department from its seat. Meetings of the Konfederalno Veće are held in Sarajevo.

art-115bis:
Confederal Chancellery 
The Confederal Chancellery (Konfederalna Kancelarija) is the general staff of the Konfederalno Veće. It is directed by the Chancellor of the Confederation (kancelar Konfederacije), elected by the Confederal Assembly by absolute majority on the proposal of the Konfederalno Veće, for a term of four years renewable once. 
The Confederal Chancellery: 
organises confederal elections and votes and records their results; 
verifies the signatures of popular initiatives and referendum requests, as well as requests made by the States; 
keeps the national registers, separately from the register of citizens (Article 14); 
publishes the Official Journal. 
Each year it publishes, for each national register, the total number of citizens enrolled on 1 January, which serves to calculate the double key (Articles 110 and 111), together with the number of registrations, rectifications, changes of register and withdrawals. Any national delegation may apply to the Ustavni Sud if it considers that concerted registrations distort the representation of its people; the Ustavni Sud may deprive the registrations concerned of effect for the following election.

art-118:
Competences of the Ustavni Sud 
The Ustavni Sud: 
rules on the conformity of confederal and State laws with the Constitution; 
settles conflicts of competence between the Confederation and the States, and between States; 
rules on individual complaints of violation of fundamental rights; 
rules on disputes concerning the rights of autonomous entities; 
rules on the validity of popular initiatives and referendums; 
rules on the regularity of confederal elections; 
rules on complaints of breach of financial equality and of manifest disproportion in confederal investment; 
authorises and supervises, through a special chamber, investigations of the Konfederalni Istražni Biro targeting confederal or State elected officials, members of State governments and judges, or bearing on the competences of a State, as well as the collection measures of the Konfederalna Obavěštajna Agencija on the territory of the Confederation (Article 145 ter), and grants the authorisations provided for in Article 101 bis; 
rules on complaints concerning the action of the confederal security forces and of the Konfederalna Obavěštajna Agencija which infringe fundamental rights or the rights of a constituent people, after the procedure of Article 138 bis; 
rules on the qualification of laws with regard to the right of mandatory consent (Article 110 bis) and of revision proposals with regard to the entrenched guarantees (Article 150); 
reviews the declaration and extension of a state of emergency and the measures taken under Article 146; 
rules on complaints concerning the national registers (Article 115 bis); 
finds failures of the States to comply with confederal law (Article 50 bis); 
interprets the Constitution in the last instance. 
Decisions of the Ustavni Sud are final and binding on all organs of the Confederation and of the States.

art-119bis:
Confederal Court of Justice 
The Confederal Court of Justice (Konfederalni Sud) is the supreme judicial authority of the Confederation, subject to the competences of the Ustavni Sud. 
It hears: 
appeals against decisions of State courts concerning the application of confederal law; 
appeals against decisions of confederal authorities; 
in the last instance, offences within the competence of the Konfederalni Istražni Biro (Article 141); 
other cases assigned to it by law, where appropriate as the sole instance (Article 32). 
The law sets the number of its judges and may establish confederal courts of first instance. The composition of the Court reflects the seven constituent peoples. Judges are elected by the Kongres for six years; they may be re-elected. 
The judges of the Confederal Court of Justice exercise their office in full independence.

art-123:
Mandatory referendum 
The following are mandatorily submitted to the vote of the people and of the States: 
amendments to the Confederal Constitution; 
accession of the Confederation to collective security organisations or to supranational communities; 
confederal laws declared urgent which lack a constitutional basis and whose period of validity exceeds one year (Article 123 bis). 
Adoption requires a double majority: a majority of voters and a majority of the States.

art-132:
Equalisation formula 
Confederal law sets the equalisation formula according to the following principles: 
equalisation is calculated from objective and publicly verifiable indicators: population, gross domestic product per inhabitant, fiscal capacity, and indicators of need (density, geography, socio-demographic burdens); 
no State may, after equalisation, have tax resources per inhabitant below eighty-five per cent of the confederal average; 
no contributing State may be deprived, by way of equalisation, of more than thirty per cent of the surplus of its fiscal capacity over the confederal average; 
the equalisation formula is revised every six years, after review by the Ustavni Sud of its conformity with constitutional principles; 
any amendment to the equalisation formula is subject to the mandatory consent of the Narodni Sabor under Article 109.

art-139:
Missions 
The Konfederalna Žandarmerija is competent for: 
the surveillance and policing of the external borders of the Confederation, carried out by the Granična Straža; 
customs and the fight against cross-border trafficking, carried out by the Granična Straža; 
the policing of transport routes, waterways and structures of confederal interest; 
the protection of confederal infrastructure, buildings and personnel, including in the confederal territories; 
the policing of the confederal autonomous municipalities (Brčko, Mostar); 
the protection of the environment and of natural areas of confederal interest; 
reinforcement of State police forces, at the express request of the State concerned and for a fixed period. 
Except in the case provided for in point g, the Konfederalna Žandarmerija may not act on the territory of a State for ordinary police missions. During a state of emergency, however, an ordinance adopted under Article 146 may deploy it there without the request of the State, for an area and a period that the ordinance sets, with the approval of the Narodni Sabor by a majority in at least five of the seven national delegations. In that context it may not be used against political, associative or trade-union gatherings.

art-141:
Nature and enumerated competence 
The Konfederalni Istražni Biro is an investigative agency without community-policing competence. It performs no territorial patrolling function. 
Its competence is strictly limited to the following offences: 
offences against the security of the Confederation and its institutions; 
terrorism; 
organised crime extending over the territory of two or more States; 
corruption of confederal officials and elected representatives, and misappropriation of confederal funds; 
offences against the integrity of confederal elections and confederal referendums; 
crimes against humanity and war crimes falling within the competence of the Confederation. 
This list may be amended only by an amendment to the Constitution (Articles 110 and 123). No ordinary law may extend the competence of the Konfederalni Istražni Biro. 
For any offence outside this list, the Konfederalni Istražni Biro has no competence and refers the matter to the State police forces.

art-145bis:
Konfederalna Obavěštajna Agencija 
The Konfederalna Obavěštajna Agencija (Confederal Intelligence Agency) collects and analyses the information necessary for the security of the Confederation. It has its seat in Sarajevo and comprises an external branch and an internal branch. 
The external branch gathers intelligence abroad. It carries out no collection activity on the territory of the Confederation. 
The internal branch acts only to prevent: 
espionage and foreign interference; 
terrorism; 
the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction; 
attacks on the critical infrastructure and information systems of the Confederation. 
This list may be amended only by an amendment to the Constitution (Articles 110 and 123). 
The Agency has no police powers: it may not arrest, search or question under compulsion. Where it discovers an offence, it informs the Konfederalni Istražni Biro or the police of the competent State.

art-150:
Entrenched guarantees 
The following provisions may be revised only with the approval of the Narodni Sabor by a two-thirds majority in plenary assembly and a majority within each of the seven national delegations: 
the parity of the constituent peoples in the Narodni Sabor and the Konfederalno Veće (Articles 107 and 111); 
the right of mandatory consent, the alarm-bell procedure and conciliation (Articles 109 a and b, 110 and 110 ter); 
human dignity, equality, the right to life and personal liberty (Articles 8, 9 and 11); 
free enrolment in the national registers, the prohibition of any compulsion to declare one's national affiliation and the right to belong to no register (Article 102); 
the attachment of each territorial State to the constituent people to which it belongs (Article 1 bis); any change to it also requires the approval of the electorate of the State concerned; 
this Article. 
This approval is required for any revision, total or partial, whether it originates with the people or with the authorities. It is in addition to acceptance by the people and the States. A popular initiative that affects an entrenched guarantee is submitted to the Narodni Sabor before the vote, within the time limit set by law; failing approval, it is not put to the vote. 
When transmitting a revision proposal or a popular initiative to the Narodni Sabor, the President of the Kongres states whether it affects an entrenched guarantee. Any national delegation may challenge this statement before the Ustavni Sud under the conditions of Article 110 bis.
